      "text": "Hal has to change the batteries every 5 months. To find out the month when he will change them for the 25th time, let's go step-by-step.\nStep 1: Determine how many times he needs to change the batteries. Since he changes them every 5 months, his total change will always be one battery replacement time.\nStep 2: Identify the battery replacement month. \n\nGo to May. We can check to see if that's incorrect, but Hal hasn't changed them yet, so they should be changed in this month.\nStep 3: Count the months until the 25th battery replacement. \n\nSince he changes the battery every 5 months, the 1st replacement will occur in May, the 2nd in October, the 3rd in the following April, and so on. Using this pattern, we can calculate how many months it will take for him to reach the 25th replacement.\n\nCalculate the 25th month:\n\nTotal months = Battery replacements × Months between replacements\nTotal months = 25 × 6 (since 6 months = 3 replacements)\n\nNow, add the first month to the total months:\n\nTotal months after first month = 25 × 6 + 5 (Months for one full replacement cycle)\n\nFinal month = [Total months after first month] in May + [Months for one full replacement cycle]\n\nThere are 3 other months (March, April, and December) between adding the additional month and the first replacement month. A similar calculation will have to run through those 3 months until the 25th replacement is reached.\n\nIn order to stretch this out as far as possible, the additional 25 months (in), plus the 3 months between adding to December, equals April.\n\nSo the answer is April.",
